WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.655
Hi, I'm Loopy!

00:00:02.655 --> 00:00:05.803
This paper puts a world model inside a self-improvement loop.

00:00:05.803 --> 00:00:07.693
Here is how that loop works.

00:00:11.058 --> 00:00:14.745
The idea: predict each candidate's outcome, pay for real feedback only on

00:00:14.745 --> 00:00:18.432
the best one, and keep an update only if policy and world

00:00:18.432 --> 00:00:20.275
model still work as a pair.

00:00:21.758 --> 00:00:23.574
The policy proposes a few candidates.

00:00:23.574 --> 00:00:26.298
The world model predicts what each one would do.

00:00:26.718 --> 00:00:30.613
Only the best predicted candidate is executed, so costly feedback goes where

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:31.911
it should pay off.

00:00:32.331 --> 00:00:34.698
The outcome is stored beside its prediction.

00:00:34.698 --> 00:00:39.095
It trains the policy, and retrains the world model if predictions have drifted.

00:00:39.515 --> 00:00:42.102
Then the gate: the new pair must beat the old pair,

00:00:42.102 --> 00:00:43.042
or both roll back.

00:00:44.608 --> 00:00:48.496
Update the parts separately and they can break each other: a new

00:00:48.496 --> 00:00:51.412
policy proposes actions the old world model never saw.

00:00:51.832 --> 00:00:53.701
So the pair is judged as one system.

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:01.616
In the paper's figure, the top band is one round of interaction.

00:01:02.036 --> 00:01:04.790
Below it, the checks that decide which pair carries forward.

00:01:06.208 --> 00:01:09.888
The GUI experiment tests every pairing of old and new policy with

00:01:09.888 --> 00:01:11.421
old and new world model.

00:01:11.841 --> 00:01:14.766
Under the reported controller only the policy row matters.

00:01:14.766 --> 00:01:17.366
The gate had switched the world model off.

00:01:17.786 --> 00:01:22.185
Rank actions with the world model instead, and the updated world column wins.

00:01:23.508 --> 00:01:27.367
In code repair, the world model ranks a frozen coder's patches.

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:31.577
Better ranking lifts hidden-test success, and retraining it adds a little more.

00:01:33.058 --> 00:01:37.307
The lesson: a world model earns its place by choosing what gets real feedback.

00:01:37.307 --> 00:01:39.128
Crossed tests show whether it did.

00:01:41.108 --> 00:01:42.745
Predict, then pay for one outcome.

00:01:42.745 --> 00:01:43.564
Gate the pair.

00:01:43.564 --> 00:01:44.655
And test it crossed.

00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:47.800
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:48.398
I'm Loopy.

00:01:48.398 --> 00:01:48.697
Bye!
